    {
      "id": 30,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        40,
        560
      ],
      "size": [
        400,
        420
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 16,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "Note"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "## What each step buys\n\n**AtlasDepthMap** — `moge_tile_side` (0 = off) runs inference on overlapping\ntiles at SOURCE resolution instead of letting the model downscale a 36MP plate\nto its token budget. Every tile is affine-fitted onto one global pass first,\nbecause monocular depth is scale-ambiguous per input and raw tiles step at\nevery seam. Costs one pass per tile plus the global one.\n\n**AtlasMogeNormals** — runs a MoGe `*-normal` model purely for its normals and\nthrows away its depth, so you keep V2's far-field (which behaves on exteriors,\nwhere MoGe's runs away) AND get MoGe's cleaner normals.\n\n**AtlasDepthDetailEnhance** — embosses those normals' high frequencies onto the\ndepth. Monocular depth is metrically sound but low-frequency: brick courses,\nwindow reveals and rock striations flatten out, and this puts them back."
      ],
      "title": "THE DEPTH CHAIN",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 31,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        2240,
        800
      ],
      "size": [
        420,
        440
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 17,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "Note"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "## The widened camera\n\n`frame_outpaint_px` on the clean-plate layer can already widen a plate past the\nframe edge — its own tooltip calls the frame-edge reveal *\"the binding\nconstraint on wide scenes\"*. But the ring is edge-replicated smear and depth\ncould not follow it.\n\n**AtlasOutpaintDepth** re-runs depth on the widened plate and stitches it to the\noriginal, affine-fitting the widened pass onto the region they share first: a\nmonocular model returns a DIFFERENT scale on a different framing, so pasting\nwould step at the frame boundary.\n\n### Note the wiring\nThe lower relief mesh is fed `widened_solve`, **not** the original solve. Every\ngeometry node reads width/cx/cy from the SOLVE rather than from the depth map,\nso the original camera would back-project the new ring against the old principal\npoint — misregistering it by exactly the padding, while looking entirely\nplausible.\n\n### Note the OTHER wiring\nThe outpaint branch takes depth from **AtlasDepthMap (raw)**, not from the\ndetail-enhanced output. This node re-runs a raw pass on the widened plate and\naffine-fits it onto whatever depth it is handed — so handing it POST-PROCESSED\ndepth means the two differ in high-frequency content, which an affine fit cannot\nabsorb. Measured on this plate: enhanced input produced a residual of 7.7% of\nscene depth and tripped the node's own warning. Raw in, raw out, enhance after.\n\n### The ring is invented\n`ImagePadForOutpaint` here is edge replication, not generation. For real content\nput a prompt-driven inpaint between the pad and the depth node; the geometry\nfollows whatever the pixels say."
      ],
      "title": "WHY THE LOWER BRANCH EXISTS",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
